;;; on the user's ruling of that day (Q-M1e2.20 (a)).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(holds "1")`: the loader holds a string written as a fact's
;;; argument as the name it spells, so this is the name `1`, a value apart
;;; from the int `1` (`01_grammar.md`'s `STRING` row). The query's `:expect`
;;; claims the one class `(model (holds 1))`, written with the int. The claim
;;; is the witness: it is false, and it stays written as it is.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `holds` is the one relation with a signature and `1` the one name, so H
;;; proposes nothing root does not hold, and R is one state, root's. The goal
;;; `(holds ?v)` has one row there, `?v` the name `1`, so the query has one
;;; class, `(model (holds "1"))`. The claim lists `(model (holds 1))`, a fact
;;; over the int, which no state holds: the claim is FAILED, and `ein solve`
;;; and `ein test` exit 1, as a refuted claim does.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `86227f9a` a claim was compared with the state as printed text, and the
;;; printer printed a name as its raw text, so the int `1` and the name `"1"`
;;; were one text, `(holds 1)`: `ein test -v FILE` said `holds` and exited 0,
;;; in a release build and a debug one.

(relation holds T)
(holds "1")
(query :goal (holds ?v) :expect (model (holds 1)))
